1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now it's to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Hey, let me take you back a couple years ago to Camp Olatto out there in California.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Troop 14 is getting ready for bed.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
All of a sudden, there's a shout from inside one of the tents,

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
and there's some scuffling, and then the plaintiff cried,

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Mr. Underwood, I think we have a problem.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
So Mr. Underwood asked what kind of problem,

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,000
but there were just some more muffled thumps and some more scuffling.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
After a moment, he said, did you take care of it?

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
And the scouts replied, yeah, it was a nickel.

11
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Yeah, it was a nickel?

12
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Well, as Walter Underwood, who's a scout leader out there with Troop 14,

13
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
reconstructed it, one of the scouts got into his sleeping bag

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
and his barefoot touched a cold nickel.

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
You know, like a coin.

16
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
A nickel coin.

17
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Walter says that he's not sure what the scout thought that nickel was,

18
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
but it made him jump.

19
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
He jumped out and stomped the nickel until it wasn't dangerous anymore.

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Hey, I guess we can be happy it wasn't a quarter or a deadly 50 cents.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Hey, this is Podcast Number 61.

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Hey, welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

23
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
This is Clark Green.

24
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Hey, thanks, Walter, for the story.

25
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
That was a story that Walter Underwood sent me by email.

26
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Yeah, the one that you heard just before the opening music there.

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Walter is a longtime listener and reader of the Scoutmaster blog,

28
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
and I sure do appreciate you getting in touch.

29
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,000
You can get in touch.

30
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
You can send me stories and questions and emails.

31
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
That's right, by email or over the phone,

32
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
and you'll find out how to do that right here at the end of the podcast.

33
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
So let's see what came across this week.

34
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Don left a review on iTunes for the podcast.

35
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
He says, one of the best podcasts on scouting.

36
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
I enjoy them from beginning to end.

37
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Well, thank you very much, Don, for taking the trouble to do that.

38
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
If you subscribe through iTunes, take a moment,

39
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
go over and leave a review or a rating about the Scoutmaster Podcast.

40
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Sure would appreciate it.

41
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
I heard from John Sloan.

42
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
He said, in April, I'm taking over as Scoutmaster for the troop where I earned my eagle.

43
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Hey, good for you, John.

44
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,000
I've only recently gotten back involved with scouts,

45
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
and I'm trying very hard to get as informed as I can.

46
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Your podcasts are fantastic for that.

47
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
I'm beginning to feel prepared to be a true mentor for our youth leadership.

48
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Thanks so much for taking the time to put this information out there for people like me.

49
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Well, thanks, John, for getting in touch, and thank you for the kind words.

50
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
And I'm very interested to hear how things go.

51
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Yeah, let me know after you take over in April how things are going.

52
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
A lot of new Scoutmasters listen to this podcast.

53
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
A lot of people who are preparing to become Scoutmasters are assistant Scoutmasters.

54
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
So we can all benefit from each other's experience.

55
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Dave wrote in and said this.

56
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Thanks again, Clark, for a great podcast.

57
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
I've spread the word about you to my fellow Scouters.

58
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
I never fail to harvest some gem from you each week.

59
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Well, that's very kind of you, Dave. Thank you.

60
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Your podcast on incoming Weeblos has me wondering, and that was last week's podcast, podcast number 60.

61
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
We talked a little bit about Weeblos transition time.

62
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:03,000
This spring, we welcomed no new Weeblos, not one.

63
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
We did have five from my former pack that camped with us last year at a camperee,

64
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
and they seemed to have a great time, but they chose to go to another troop.

65
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
And that troop has many years of history and a long list of Eagle Scouts.

66
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,000
In comparison, we're kind of small and struggling a little bit.

67
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Now, I had two questions for you.

68
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:28,000
With the new national journey to excellence standards that result in a numerical rating,

69
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
how will smaller troops have a chance to recruit?

70
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
The second question Dave had is,

71
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
when we invite local Weeblos to troop meetings,

72
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:42,000
I receive a lot of resentment from other Scoutmasters who feel entitled to their feeder packs, quote unquote.

73
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
How do we recruit Scouts in that kind of a climate?

74
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Well, Dave, a couple of quick thoughts.

75
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The journey to excellence that Dave's mentions, most of you will be familiar with that if you're not.

76
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
There was years ago something called the Quality Unit Award,

77
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
and it was just an evaluation that was done, a self-evaluation of the troop program.

78
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:10,000
And if you met so many criteria, you were judged to be a quality unit.

79
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
That turned into, a couple of years ago, the Centennial Quality Unit Award,

80
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
or I forget exactly what the terminology was.

81
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
And that was a program that went on for a couple of years,

82
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and it was found to be, for a lot of people, it was kind of confusing.

83
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,000
I know it was for me.

84
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
And now, this year, BSA is rolling out the journey to excellence,

85
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
which looks to me like a pretty good evaluation of a troops program.

86
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,000
I like this one better than I like either of the previous permutations.

87
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,000
So there's what the journey to excellence is.

88
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
And Dave, I don't know if that's going to become a really important part of the way people evaluate.

89
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
You know, Weeblos parents evaluate troops.

90
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:02,000
I can tell you probably that Weeblos themselves are going to have no to little idea about those numbers,

91
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
and they're probably not going to care about them all that much.

92
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:09,000
What they're going to care about is what the scouts, who the scouts and the troop are,

93
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
and how they are treated when they visited.

94
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:19,000
And I'll be interested to hear if that becomes like a default kind of numerical standard by which people judge troops.

95
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
I don't know that the quality unit ever earned us any Weeblos.

96
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
I don't know if the Centennial Quality Unit thing ever earned us any Weeblos.

97
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
And I don't know if the journey to excellence will either.

98
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
I think what's more important is the kind of relationship that you form with a pack

99
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and the kind of work that you do in recruiting them into the troop.

100
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
I think that puts you on a pretty even playing field.

101
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Your second question about getting resentment from other scout masters who feel entitled to Weeblos somehow.

102
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Yeah, that doesn't wash with me at all.

103
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
I'll tell you, I don't like that attitude.

104
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
I've seen it before.

105
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
You'll win, Dave.

106
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:08,000
If you're going toe-to-toe with guys with an attitude like that, you're going to win because you don't have that attitude, right?

107
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:15,000
What we really need to have as volunteers in scouting is an attitude of community scouting.

108
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:21,000
If boys join another pack or another troop or another venture crew other than the one that we're associated with,

109
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
we should be pleased because they're getting the benefits of the program.

110
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
I mean, that's what it's all about, right?

111
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Just because they don't happen to join our program shouldn't be a cause for resentment.

112
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:39,000
And the fact that people are competing for their attention when they're Weeblos,

113
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
I don't know why that would be a cause of resentment.

114
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
So, Dave, I don't know what to tell you,

115
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:53,000
but other than I just don't think that that's a very scout-like attitude for scout leaders to have.

116
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:56,000
And I know you don't have that attitude.

117
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,000
And if you just rise above it, people will notice that you have risen above it.

118
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
I'm sorry that that's happening to you,

119
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:10,000
but it's one of those little scouting dramas that sometimes are best ignored, you know?

120
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:16,000
So I hope that helps, Dave, and I'd like to continue discussing the whole subject with you.

121
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:20,000
This time around, this time around in Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less,

122
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,000
we're going to talk about deep philosophical meanings.

123
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:28,000
That's right. I mean, get ready.

124
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:35,000
And then we have an email that came in that I want to take a little time to answer.

125
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
And I think that's enough for any podcast, don't you?

126
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
Well, let's get started, shall we?

127
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,000
There are things we know we know.

128
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Okay, I got that.

129
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
And we also know there are known unknowns.

130
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
That is to say, we know there are some things we do not know.

131
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:01,000
I think I do.

132
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
But there are also unknown unknowns.

133
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,000
What?

134
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
The ones we don't know, we don't know.

135
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,000
What?

136
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
Excuse me, but is this an unknown unknown?

137
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Yeah, really. What is he talking about?

138
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
I'm not several unknowns, and I just want to make this an unknown unknown.

139
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
I'm not going to say which it is.

140
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
That was Eagle Scout Donald Rumsfeld when he was the Secretary of Defense.

141
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
We're not going to go into politics here,

142
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,000
but I think it's an important little piece, a little statement that was made,

143
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,000
that got repeated often and people puzzled over it.

144
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Actually, I think some of the concepts that are there

145
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,000
are probably a good thing for us to talk about as scout leaders.

146
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Yeah, no, really.

147
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
You know, stick with me for a moment.

148
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Because what was contained in there is part of what psychologists call

149
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
the four stages of competence.

150
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,000
So let's just review those quickly, shall we?

151
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
Stage number one is unconscious incompetence.

152
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Stage number two is conscious incompetence.

153
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Stage number three is, can you guess, conscious competence.

154
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
And stage number four, unconscious competence.

155
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:16,000
Now, what in the world does that have to do with the price of tea in China,

156
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,000
you may be asking?

157
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Well, I will tell you exactly what it has to do with scouting.

158
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:28,000
We're dealing and working with developing youth responsibility,

159
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
developing youth leadership.

160
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:37,000
And most of them come to us at the first stage of competence.

161
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Unconscious incompetence.

162
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
I mean, they don't know what they don't know.

163
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:49,000
They don't understand or know how to do something.

164
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:55,000
And they don't recognize that they don't know how to do it.

165
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
They don't even necessarily know that it exists.

166
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
That's really important for us to know, isn't it?

167
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:05,000
If we base our evaluation on the idea that we have a whole lot of shared

168
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
experience and a whole lot of shared knowledge with the guys in our troop

169
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:15,000
who are 14, 13, 14, 15 years old and who we are entrusting with real

170
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:19,000
responsibility, we're in for a little bit of a shock, aren't we?

171
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Because they are unconsciously incompetent.

172
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
I'm not saying anything bad about them.

173
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
They just don't know what they don't know.

174
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:33,000
So they begin not knowing what they don't know.

175
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:37,000
And then we want to get them to stage number two, which is conscious incompetence.

176
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:42,000
Conscious incompetence does not understand or know how to do something,

177
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:48,000
but they recognize the fact that they don't know how to do it.

178
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
So that's pretty important.

179
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:54,000
The third stage we want to get them to is conscious competence.

180
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
In other words, we want them to be able to demonstrate the skill or

181
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:01,000
knowledge with consciousness and concentration.

182
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:06,000
I mean, training scouts is leading through this progression of competence,

183
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
these stages of competence.

184
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
Before anybody progresses through these stages, they have to first

185
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:14,000
encounter the Dunning-Kruger effect.

186
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:17,000
I'm sure you're all familiar with the Dunning-Kruger effect.

187
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Dunning-Kruger effect was put forward about 10 years ago by Justin Kruger

188
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and David Dunning.

189
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Basically, the effect describes that those with little knowledge or skill tend

190
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,000
to overestimate their skills or knowledge.

191
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:32,000
And those with more knowledge or skill actually tend to underestimate their

192
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:33,000
capabilities.

193
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:39,000
If you're at the stage of unconscious incompetence, you're probably going to

194
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
overestimate your abilities.

195
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Now, who does this sound like?

196
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Does this sound like you're a senior patrol leader?

197
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Does this sound like a patrol leader?

198
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
They overestimate their skills or knowledge sometimes.

199
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:56,000
They need to have a very realistic estimation of what they don't know.

200
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
That's what training is.

201
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:03,000
Getting people to stage number two of conscious incompetence.

202
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,000
There are things that I don't know about that I need to know.

203
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
So, how do we get this to happen?

204
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
How do we make this happen?

205
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
Well, we ask a lot of questions.

206
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,000
Here's an example.

207
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:18,000
We just attended a bridge-crossing ceremony yesterday.

208
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:24,000
There's a new patrol leader who's there to accept these new weeblos who are

209
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,000
leaving Cub Scouts coming over into the troop.

210
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,000
And I asked him, I said, what do you want to know about these guys?

211
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:39,000
And he kind of looked at me and he said, well, I really don't know.

212
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,000
So we were at stage one, unconscious incompetence.

213
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
What do you mean, what do I want to know about these guys?

214
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:49,000
I said, well, are you going to contact them any time?

215
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
Yeah, I am.

216
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
What do you need to know to do that?

217
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:57,000
Oh, I need to know what their phone numbers are.

218
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
I said, well, I'd begin with their names.

219
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:03,000
You probably need to know their names, but you also need to know their phone numbers.

220
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,000
He got it.

221
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,000
He was consciously incompetent.

222
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Wow, I didn't think about that.

223
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,000
And then he went and got names and phone numbers.

224
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:15,000
There's a couple of approaches there that could have been taken in that particular scenario.

225
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:19,000
I could have gotten the names and the phone numbers of the new Scouts for him

226
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:22,000
and given them to him.

227
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
Now, that would have been a nice thing for me to do,

228
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
but it probably wouldn't have been the most optimum way to make this happen

229
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:33,000
because my new patrol leader wouldn't have really progressed very far.

230
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:38,000
He would have a paper with names and phone numbers on it.

231
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:42,000
I could tell him, go get everybody's name and phone number

232
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,000
because you're going to need to call them.

233
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
That would have been better than me just giving them to him.

234
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:53,000
But also, right then, I'm kind of skipping over the idea that

235
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
this is something that he needs to be thinking about.

236
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
So the third scenario, the one that I just described,

237
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
is asking him the question, what do you need to know about these guys?

238
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
And are you going to contact them any time?

239
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
That makes him think.

240
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:10,000
That makes him discover what he does not know

241
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:15,000
and then move ahead to the next stage to begin to address it.

242
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
And that particular skill is much, much more important

243
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
than him having a set of names and telephone numbers.

244
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
So let's review this very quickly.

245
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
There are four stages of competence.

246
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
There's unconscious incompetence.

247
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
In other words, somebody does not know what they don't know.

248
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Then there's conscious incompetence.

249
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
They don't necessarily understand something,

250
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:38,000
but they recognize that they don't understand it.

251
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,000
And then stage number three is conscious competence,

252
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:44,000
where they're able to demonstrate the skill or knowledge

253
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,000
with consciousness and concentration.

254
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,000
And stage number four, by the way, we didn't discuss very much,

255
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
but is unconscious competence.

256
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:58,000
And that is being so well practiced in the skill or knowledge

257
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
that it's kind of second nature.

258
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:04,000
And a person at this stage is able to teach others the skill.

259
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
There's a number of things in life that you and I as adults

260
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:11,000
are unconsciously competent about.

261
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:15,000
Those things correspond to things that a lot of our youth leaders

262
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
are still at stage one.

263
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
They don't know that they don't know them.

264
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:25,000
So it's our job to help them begin to understand that

265
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:29,000
and to get them to move on through the stages of competence.

266
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
I mentioned the Dunning-Kruger effect again.

267
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
And just to review, that's the phenomenon

268
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
that those with little knowledge or skill

269
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,000
tend to overestimate their skills or knowledge.

270
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
And those with more knowledge or skill

271
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:45,000
actually tend to underestimate their capabilities.

272
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
One of the most important things we can do as scout leaders

273
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:51,000
is help our scouts develop the internal process

274
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,000
of going through these stages of competence

275
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
and gaining skill and knowledge

276
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
and then learning not to overestimate that skill and knowledge,

277
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,000
but being open to developing them and to working on them.

278
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
We do that in several different ways.

279
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
Probably the most effective one

280
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
is by asking them a lot of leading questions

281
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and getting them to discover a lot of these things for themselves.

282
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Hi, this is Dr. Paul Auerbach

283
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
and you are listening to Clark Green

284
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
on the Scoutmaster Podcast.

285
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
Email that is, folks.

286
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:52,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

287
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:02,000
Hey, I have this email from

288
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:07,000
Assistant Scoutmaster Michael Vino of Troop 2000

289
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
in Brockton, Mass.

290
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:10,000
He says this,

291
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
I've been told scouts can't use power tools,

292
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:15,000
but the BSA guidelines I read say they can.

293
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
The only thing is is that they're completely void

294
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
of any specific information as to which tools are allowed

295
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
and which ones aren't.

296
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
I need to know this because it will determine

297
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:27,000
how I'm going to counsel some merit badges.

298
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
And it will determine which ones I'm going to counsel

299
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
and which ones I'm not.

300
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
Where can I go to get the requested info?

301
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
Well, Mike, first of all, thanks for getting in touch.

302
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
This is a pretty easy question to answer.

303
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
Go to the Guide to Safe Scouting

304
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
and there will be a link to that guide

305
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:45,000
in the post that contains this podcast online.

306
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
And you're going to find in the Guide to Safe Scouting

307
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:49,000
a chart.

308
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
And that chart is going to say

309
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
that anyone who is a Boy Scout

310
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
that's ten and a half years or older

311
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
and registered as a Boy Scout

312
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
is permitted to use power tools.

313
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
You should note that the guide is silent

314
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:04,000
on which power tools they get to use

315
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
because that is going to be up

316
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:09,000
to whoever is running the activity.

317
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
I'm a carpenter by trade

318
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,000
and I have a lot of familiarity with the subject.

319
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
Now, I don't have a problem with most 11-year-olds

320
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,000
operating a battery power drill, you know,

321
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
with proper supervision and proper training.

322
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
That's not a problem.

323
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
But I'm not going to really...

324
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
There's not too many 11-year-olds

325
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
that I have using a circular saw.

326
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
Now, that being said,

327
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
there are some 17-year-old Scouts

328
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:36,000
who worry me when they use something like a butter knife

329
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:39,000
and I wouldn't let them within ten feet of a circular saw.

330
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
And that's the reason that there isn't

331
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:45,000
a specific listing of who gets to do what.

332
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:48,000
You're supposed to, as an adult,

333
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
use your experience and common sense

334
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:53,000
to determine who's going to be able to

335
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
use what power tool.

336
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
And you're going to train them properly

337
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
and you're going to give them

338
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
all of the safety information they need.

339
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
And you're going to be backed up in that

340
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
because all of the craft merit badges

341
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
that would use power tools,

342
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
they have safety elements built in around that.

343
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
Now, before we talk about that,

344
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
I do need to mention that

345
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
any one of the craft merit badges,

346
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
you know, woodworking, wood carving,

347
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
you know, leather work, all of those,

348
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
all of those can be completed with hand tools.

349
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,000
And I kind of prefer it that way

350
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
because it kind of slows down the process

351
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
and you get to know what you're doing better.

352
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:32,000
It's a little slower, but it can be completed.

353
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:35,000
And you'll also note that there's a safety element

354
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
built into all of those merit badges.

355
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:40,000
Woodworking merit badge says this,

356
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:43,000
show that you know first aid for injuries

357
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
and also tell what precautions must be taken

358
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
to help prevent the loss of eyesight or hearing

359
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:50,000
and explain why and when it's necessary

360
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:53,000
to use a dust mask and then tell your counselor

361
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:56,000
what precautions you take to safely use your tools.

362
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:00,000
So, obviously, if you're using a dust mask

363
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
and hearing protection,

364
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
to me that would indicate that, you know,

365
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
you might end up using power tools

366
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:09,000
to fulfill this merit badge in some situations.

367
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:12,000
You know, people come up with some of these rules

368
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
off the top of their heads sometimes

369
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
because if they're not familiar with power tools,

370
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:17,000
they may think,

371
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
wow, I don't want any children using power tools.

372
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
Well, children could be trained to use them

373
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:24,000
just like they can be trained to use pocket knives.

374
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
And it can be done safely

375
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
with the right kind of supervision

376
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:29,000
and the right kind of training.

377
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
If you have any personal doubt

378
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
as to your abilities in that department,

379
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
then don't do it.

380
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
Sometimes a troop or a camp or a council

381
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:40,000
may overlay some safety rules,

382
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
whether they're advisable or inadvisable,

383
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
I'm not going to judge.

384
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:47,000
But the BSA itself does not.

385
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
So as a merit badge counselor,

386
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
as a volunteer merit badge counselor,

387
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:54,000
I wouldn't have any problem with you going out

388
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:57,000
and properly showing scouts

389
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
how to safely use power tools

390
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
and using your supervision and judgment

391
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:05,000
to assure that they don't get injured by them.

392
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
I hope that helps.

393
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:08,000
Thanks a lot, Mike,

394
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:09,000
for becoming a merit badge counselor.

395
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:10,000
It's a lot of fun.

396
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
And if I can help you with any other questions,

397
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:13,000
let me know.

398
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
You can get in touch with your questions.

399
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:16,000
That's right, you can.

400
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
Your questions, your comments, your funny stories,

401
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:19,000
you can get in touch with me

402
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
here at the Scoutmaster Podcast.

403
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
I'm going to tell you how to do that in just a moment.

404
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:50,000
Well, thanks for listening to another edition

405
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
of the Scoutmaster Podcast.

406
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
Here's how to keep in touch.

407
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
You can read the Scoutmaster blog

408
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:00,000
at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

409
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,000
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster newsletter,

410
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:06,000
which is a monthly digest of the Scoutmaster blog and podcast,

411
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
and some great scouting resources

412
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
around a monthly theme.

413
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:11,000
Every newsletter also...

414
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:12,000
newsletter?

415
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
No, newsletter,

416
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:17,000
always includes a giveaway from one of our affiliate sponsors.

417
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
This month it's from E-Lite,

418
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:24,000
and E-Lite is a concentrated electrolyte replacement product

419
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:28,000
that I think is going to be one of my standard take-alongs

420
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
every time I'm here.

421
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:43,000
You can also subscribe to the Scoutmaster Podcast on iTunes

422
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
and when you do, feel free to leave a comment

423
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
or a review or a rating.

424
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
And you can call

425
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
and leave a voicemail comment or question

426
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:54,000
that could be included in the Scoutmaster Podcast.

427
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
That's right. How about that?

428
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
You can be part of the podcast.

429
00:23:58,000 --> 00:23:59,000
Call this number,

430
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:04,000
484-734-0002.

431
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:11,000
484-734-0002.

432
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
And you'll hear a voicemail announcement.

433
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
Then you go ahead and record your message

434
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:19,000
and you might hear yourself in the next Scoutmaster Podcast.

435
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:23,000
You can follow us at ScoutmasterBlog on Facebook

436
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:26,000
and ScoutmasterCG on Twitter.

437
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
And finally, you can email me, Clark Green,

438
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
with your comments and questions at

439
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:34,000
clarkgreen.gmail.com

440
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:38,000
That's C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N

441
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
at gmail.com

442
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
Finally, let me say the Scoutmaster Blog

443
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
and the Scoutmaster Podcast are not

444
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
official publications of the Boy Scouts of America

445
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
nor are they endorsed or sponsored

446
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
by the Boy Scouts of America.

447
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
Hey, it's just me talking into a microphone

448
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:15,000
trying to lend a hand to Scout leaders

449
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,000
and maybe have a little bit of fun along the way.

450
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
And really finally this time,

451
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:33,000
before we leave you,

452
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,000
let's hear from our founder, Sir Robert Dayton Powell.

453
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:36,000
Sir Robert?

454
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:39,000
Good luck to you and good camping.

455
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:40,000
Why thank you, sir.

456
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
Until next time.

